This is an old version of `useCallbackRef` — a version that has a `useEffect` to update the ref. This is the classic buggy version! In the old implementation:

```tsx
function useCallbackRef<T extends (...args: any[]) => any>(callback: T | undefined): T {
  const callbackRef = React.useRef(callback);

  React.useEffect(() => {
    callbackRef.current = callback;
  });

  return React.useMemo(() => ((...args) => callbackRef.current?.(...args)) as T, []);
}
```

This was fixed in Radix PR #3081 ("fix(use-callback-ref): update callback synchronously") or something similar to it — the issue: because the callback is only updated inside `useEffect`, the ref callback (called during commit/render phase) ends up calling a STALE callback. In React 19, ref callbacks are called during render (for cleanup), or... hmm.

